<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Fourteenth pregnancy at 37, at 13 weeks 2 days by today&apos;s scan, conceived about five and a half months after her last birth, with iron deficiency anemia. Please advise on surveillance, anemia treatment, placental assessment, the timing and place of birth, and planning to prevent postpartum hemorrhage. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What surveillance does this pregnancy need, given her age, grand multiparity, and the short interval since her last birth?</item><item>How should her iron deficiency anemia be treated, and when should we move to intravenous iron?</item><item>How should the placenta be assessed for previa and accreta after thirteen births?</item><item>When and where should she give birth, and should labor be induced?</item><item>What should the postpartum hemorrhage prevention and treatment plan include?</item><item>What advice should she have about travel during this pregnancy?</item><item>When and how should contraception and the interval before any future pregnancy be discussed?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mumtaz Mahal is seen at the request of Dr. Qureshi-Lindholm at 17 weeks 2 days. She has had thirteen vaginal births, all by her account at or near term, with labors that were often short, and no cesarean, uterine surgery, or blood transfusion; she does not know of heavy bleeding after any birth. One baby died soon after birth, and five other children died in infancy or early childhood. This pregnancy began about five and a half months after her last birth. She has taken about half of her oral iron doses because of nausea and constipation and remains tired. She has no bleeding, pain, or urinary symptoms and has felt the first movements. She travels often with her family and expects to travel again in the spring. She wants clear plans and prefers women clinicians. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>She was born Arjumand Banu Begum in Agra in April 1593; an encyclopedia, citing a seventeenth-century court history, gives 27 April 1593. [D]</item><item>Her father, Abu&apos;l-Hasan Asaf Khan, was a Persian noble; his sister was the Empress Nur Jahan, wife of the emperor Jahangir. Her mother&apos;s family came from Qazvin in Persia. [D]</item><item>She was betrothed to Prince Khurram, later the emperor Shah Jahan, in 1607, and married him on 10 May 1612; he gave her the title Mumtaz Mahal, rendered as &apos;Chosen One of the Palace&apos; or &apos;the Exalted One of the Palace&apos;. [D]</item><item>She was a Shi&apos;a Muslim. [D]</item><item>Despite her frequent pregnancies, she travelled with her husband&apos;s entourage on his military campaigns. [D]</item><item>Her first thirteen children were born between 1613 and 1630: Hur-ul-Nisa (1613), Jahanara (1614), Dara Shikoh (1615), Shah Shuja (1616), Roshanara (1617), Aurangzeb (1618), Izad Bakhsh (1619), Surayya Banu (1621), a son who died soon after birth (1622), Murad Bakhsh (1624), Lutf Allah (1626), Daulat Afza (1628), and Husn Ara (1630). [D]</item><item>Six of her children died in infancy or early childhood before 1631: Hur-ul-Nisa at three and Surayya Banu at seven, both of smallpox; Izad Bakhsh in infancy; a son soon after birth in 1622; Lutf Allah at about eighteen months; and Daulat Afza in infancy. [D]</item><item>Her thirteenth child, Husn Ara, was born on 23 April 1630. [D]</item><item>In the year 1040 AH (from August 1630) the emperor was in the Deccan with the court at Burhanpur, during a severe famine in the Deccan and Gujarat, when he ordered soup kitchens and weekly alms for as long as he remained at Burhanpur. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

