    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Fourteenth pregnancy at 37 (O09.529). A singleton pregnancy at an advanced maternal age with a low-risk aneuploidy screen. Age 35 or older raises the risks of preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, fetal growth restriction, and stillbirth; aspirin is correctly started. [S]</item><item>Grand multiparity (O09.40). Thirteen previous births raise the risks of uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage, malpresentation, and fast labor. Without a uterine scar, the risk of placenta accreta spectrum is low unless the placenta lies over the cervix. [S]</item><item>Short interpregnancy interval (O09.899). An interval under six months from birth to conception is linked to anemia, preterm birth, and a small baby. [S]</item><item>Iron deficiency anemia in pregnancy (O99.019). Iron deficiency anemia that has not responded to oral iron, which she cannot tolerate; thalassemia trait excluded. Anemia at delivery would leave her little reserve if she bleeds. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The dating and nuchal translucency scan; cell-free DNA (low risk); blood group O RhD positive with a negative antibody screen; rubella immune; hepatitis B, HIV, syphilis, and hepatitis C negative; urine culture no growth; hemoglobin electrophoresis normal; hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL with ferritin 9 ng/mL at intake and 10.3 g/dL with ferritin 12 ng/mL today; hemoglobin A1c 5.4 percent; today&apos;s vital signs (blood pressure 106/64 mmHg, weight 59.8 kg). [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Intravenous iron now that she is in the second trimester: ferric carboxymaltose 750 mg today and again in four weeks, with hemoglobin and ferritin before each dose and at 24 to 28 weeks; aim for a hemoglobin of at least 11 g/dL before delivery. [S]</item><item>Detailed anatomy scan at 20 to 22 weeks with placental location; if the placenta is low-lying or over the cervix, repeat at 32 weeks and assess for accreta; otherwise no MRI is needed. [S]</item><item>Continue aspirin 81 mg nightly until delivery; blood pressure and urine protein at every visit; glucose challenge at 24 to 28 weeks; visits every two weeks from 28 weeks and weekly from 36 weeks; a growth scan at 32 to 34 weeks; weekly fetal heart rate monitoring from 36 weeks can be offered. [S]</item><item>Birth in a hospital with a blood bank, 24-hour anesthesia, and an operating theatre; offer induction at 39 weeks, which also avoids an unplanned birth away from the hospital. [S]</item><item>Postpartum hemorrhage plan, written by 36 weeks: risk assessment on admission, type and screen, two large-bore lines, oxytocin 10 units at birth with controlled cord traction, quantitative blood loss, and, if hemorrhage occurs, tranexamic acid 1 g intravenously within 3 hours of birth with a second dose after 30 minutes if bleeding continues, alongside uterotonics, uterine massage, and the hemorrhage protocol; anesthesia consultation at 36 weeks. [S]</item><item>Travel is reasonable in the second trimester and early third trimester if she stays well: carry her records, keep her prenatal visits where she stays, walk and drink often on long journeys, and stay near the delivery hospital from 36 weeks. [S]</item><item>Discuss postpartum contraception and the interval before any future pregnancy in the third trimester, in her own terms; advise waiting at least 18 months, ideally 24, after this birth. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Qureshi-Lindholm the same day; Mumtaz Mahal agrees with the plan, and the first iron infusion is given today. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary obstetric care continues with Dr. Qureshi-Lindholm. Maternal-fetal medicine will review the anatomy scan and see her again if any problem arises, and in any case before the birth. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
